          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is G47 a code, a category or a block, and how would I tell the difference?",
          "answer": "**G47 is a category.** It is a three-character code, and it sits inside the block G40 to G47 (episodic and paroxysmal disorders) in Chapter 6, the nervous system chapter, which runs from G00 to G99. The insomnia document describes G47 as the category for sleep disorders of organic origin. [coding-insomnia-g47; coding-icd10-chapter-06-nervous]\n\nThe sources show the levels like this:\n\n- **Chapter:** a letter range. Chapter 6 covers G00 to G99, and every code in it starts with G. [coding-icd10-chapter-06-nervous]\n- **Block:** a numeric range of categories inside a chapter, such as G40 to G47 or I10 to I16. [coding-icd10-chapter-06-nervous; coding-hypertension-i10]\n- **Category:** a three-character code, such as G47 or I10. [coding-hypertension-i10]\n- **Subcategory or longer code:** a category with extra characters added. The hypertension document says most categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character before the code is complete. [coding-hypertension-i10]\n\n**A simple check by length:** if the code is one letter and two digits, it is a category. Each extra character makes it more specific. A code that is a range, like \"G40 to G47,\" is a block. A single letter with a range of numbers is a chapter.\n\nThe sources do not describe the decimal point in detail, so I cannot confirm from them that a dot after the third character marks a subcategory like G47.0. Your coding manual or a coder can confirm the exact punctuation rules.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
